-- The broadcast team needs a clean list of events for their graphics package. Write a query that joins the events and disciplines tables to return a single column named event_display_name. This column should concatenate the discipline and the event name together, separated by a hyphen and spaces (e.g., "Swimming - 100m Freestyle").


-- 5. Window Functions (Ranking)

-- Compare the scale of the Summer and Winter games independently. Write a query using the editions table to rank the editions by total_medals awarded, partitioned by the season. Return the edition_year, season, total_medals, and their rank (where rank 1 is the highest number of medals for that season). Use DENSE_RANK() to handle any potential ties.

select 
edition_id
,season
,edition_year
,dense_rank() over (partition by season order by total_medals)
from til_data_engineering.olympics.editions

;


;
-- 6. Aggregation & HAVING Clause

-- We want to identify National Olympic Committees (NOCs) that have high participation but low medal yields. Join the nocs and results tables. Find countries (country_name) that have more than 50 total event entries recorded in the results table, but have won strictly fewer than 5 medals.


select 
country_name
,count(event_instance_id)
,count(medal)
from til_data_engineering.olympics.nocs
    left join til_data_engineering.olympics.results as res 
        on nocs.noc = res.noc_id
group by country_name
having count(event_instance_id) > 50 and count(medal) < 5

